  "label": "Single Cell Perturbation Prediction",
  "related": [
    "open-electrophysiological-e8a5",
    "model-elucidating-325c",
    "regulatory-gene-9c82"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "flow-matching-continuous-molecular-a38a",
  "summary": "New generative, in-context and cross-modal predictors arrive alongside attribution and baseline benchmarks questioning where foundation models add value.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predicting single-cell transcriptional responses to genetic and chemical perturbation, with emphasis on generalizing beyond measured conditions. Architectures diverge. CytoWave applies two-stage perturbation-centric pretraining across datasets. RELAY uses relational in-context learning, conditioning on measured control–perturbed pairs that share experimental context. GENESIS trains a generative model with an energy score to predict response distributions from covariates, approaching replicate-level reproducibility. EMBER fits lightweight probes on frozen contextualized gene embeddings for single, digenic and drug responses. GRAFT transfers genetic perturbation information to zero-shot drug-response prediction across cell types. ExPert inverts the problem, inferring causal perturbations from observed states across 12 contexts. CellCanon frames prediction, explanation and observation-driven updating as an agentic world model. FateDriver ranks candidate regulators of specified reprogramming transitions. What is new is scrutiny of these models alongside new capability. One study applies attribution analyses to scGPT, Cell2Sentence and STATE. A benchmark of five foundation models against simple baselines finds their benefit limited to specific tasks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
